# Backend

## What I am learning

How to build serious backend services: HTTP APIs, authentication and authorization, validation, error handling, configuration, background work, caching, and testing.

The technology depends on the stack chosen for the main project. The focus is on concepts that transfer between languages and frameworks.

## Why it matters

The backend is the core of the main project. Everything later (databases, Docker, CI/CD, cloud, security, observability) attaches to it. A backend that is clear, testable, and configurable is easy to deploy and debug. One that is not becomes a problem at every later stage.

> **Goal:** build backend services that are understandable, testable, maintainable, and deployable.

---

## Learning objectives

By the end of this topic I can:

- Explain the request/response lifecycle, from socket to handler and back
- Design a REST API with consistent resources, methods, status codes, and error formats
- Validate input and return useful errors
- Implement authentication and authorization correctly
- Separate configuration from code and keep secrets out of the repository
- Structure code with clear layers and dependency injection
- Use middleware for cross-cutting concerns (logging, auth, error handling)
- Run background jobs and explain asynchronous processing
- Add caching and explain its trade-offs
- Write unit and integration tests and document the API
- Explain how the service behaves in production

---

## Weekly progression

| Week | Focus |
|---|---|
| 1 | HTTP in depth, request lifecycle, first endpoints, routing |
| 2 | REST design, validation, error handling, API documentation |
| 3 | Authentication, authorization, password hashing, sessions or tokens |
| 4 | Configuration, layering, dependency injection, middleware |
| 5 | Testing: unit, integration, test data, running tests in isolation |
| 6 | Background jobs, asynchronous processing, caching |
| 7-8 | Mini-project: production-style REST API, review, and documentation |

The databases topic runs in parallel from week 3. Stage 1 of the main project (client, backend, database) is built here.

---

## Checklist

- [ ] Explain the HTTP request/response lifecycle
- [ ] Choose correct methods and status codes
- [ ] Design resource-oriented REST endpoints
- [ ] Validate request input
- [ ] Return consistent, useful error responses
- [ ] Implement authentication (registration, login)
- [ ] Hash passwords correctly
- [ ] Implement authorization (roles or ownership checks)
- [ ] Load configuration from environment variables
- [ ] Keep secrets out of Git
- [ ] Separate controllers, services, and data access
- [ ] Use dependency injection
- [ ] Write middleware (logging, auth, error handling)
- [ ] Write unit tests
- [ ] Write integration tests against the API
- [ ] Run a background job
- [ ] Add caching and explain invalidation
- [ ] Generate or write API documentation
- [ ] Add a health check endpoint

A box is ticked only when I can do or explain the task without relying entirely on a tutorial.

---

## Exercises

- [ ] Write a tiny HTTP server using only the standard library to see the raw lifecycle
- [ ] Build CRUD endpoints for one resource with proper status codes
- [ ] Add validation and return a structured error for each failure type
- [ ] Implement registration and login with hashed passwords
- [ ] Protect an endpoint so only the owner or an admin can use it
- [ ] Move all configuration to environment variables with sensible defaults
- [ ] Write middleware that logs each request with a correlation ID
- [ ] Write tests for one endpoint, including failure cases

## Labs

Each lab ends with a written note: symptom, evidence, hypothesis, root cause, fix, lesson.

- [ ] **Works locally, fails elsewhere:** the service breaks when configuration changes, find the missing or wrong setting
- [ ] **Unauthorized access:** find an endpoint that returns another user's data and fix the authorization gap
- [ ] **Slow endpoint:** measure it, find the cause, and decide whether caching is the right fix
- [ ] **Failing background job:** a job fails silently, make the failure visible and handle retries
- [ ] **Flaky test:** find why a test passes or fails depending on order or data

## Mini-project: Production-style REST API

A REST API for part of the main project domain (for example users and products) that includes:

- Authentication and role-based authorization
- Input validation and consistent error responses
- Configuration through environment variables, with no secrets in Git
- Layered structure with dependency injection
- Unit and integration tests, runnable with one command
- A background job and a cached endpoint
- A health check endpoint
- API documentation
- A README explaining the design, how to run it, and how to test it
- An ADR for the main technology choices

---

## Resources

- MDN Web Docs: HTTP
- Official documentation of the chosen language and framework
- OWASP Cheat Sheet Series (authentication, password storage, input validation)
- *Designing Data-Intensive Applications*: Martin Kleppmann (for caching and async concepts, later chapters)

## Completion criteria

- [ ] I can explain every checklist item in my own words
- [ ] I can add a new endpoint with validation, auth, and tests without a tutorial
- [ ] I can debug a basic failure using the debugging methodology
- [ ] The mini-project is delivered with a README and documented decisions
- [ ] I can explain each important component: what it does, why it exists, the alternatives, and what can go wrong
- [ ] I reviewed the key concepts without AI
- [ ] Weekly reviews are written
